A||||11||80's BOY||Rear Brake disconnection ( for burnouts not sabotage)||||14||
Z||000000||80's BOY||12-21-2000||10:21 PM||||i want to do burnouts when i race at the local drag, but my suspension setup only gives me full traction, which is good in most cases. only i don't want to look stupid TRYING to burnout if i can't at all. i had a friend that disconnected his rear lines only to race with. is this possible? if so, is it better to disconnect at the master cyl. or at the wheels? anyone ever seen this done?||||reg||14
Z||000001||Archie||12-21-2000||10:27 PM||||The easiest & safest way would be to install a "Line Lock".<P>Archie<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||80's BOY||12-21-2000||10:28 PM||||mine's an auto, would that work?? BTW i hardly ever get the car out of the garage, only for some scca runs but not alot on the 1/4 mile track.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-21-2000||10:44 PM||||Yes. The lock goes in the front brake circuit for this application.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Xtreme Fiero's||12-21-2000||11:14 PM||||If you have trouble Breaking the tires loose on a launch you won't need to do a burnout. They only put head in slick to get them sticky, Radials won't do much more than wear out.||||reg||4
Z||000005||Cooter||12-21-2000||11:19 PM||||The bad part about the burnouts on radials is that a lot of them get "greasy" when over heated instead of sticky. I usually just drive around the bleach box, do a couple of dry hops and stage. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||80's BOY||12-21-2000||11:24 PM||||yeah, but i was hoping for a nice picture..you know how it goes (sigh). oh well, i'll probably just drive around the box and let em wonder.<BR>||||reg||1
